  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nOur approach fundamentally changes the optimization strategy by combining symmetry enforcement with a multi-resolution optimization scheme and Bayesian optimization. Unlike the previous method that primarily used coordinate descent and subgradient methods on a fixed grid, we implement a hierarchical optimization process that transfers solutions between different grid resolutions. This leverages the hierarchical structure of the function space and uses Bayesian optimization for global exploration while refining solutions locally. The key novelfeatures include:\n\n1. Symmetry reduction: We enforce h(2-x)=1-h(x), eliminating half the variables and signifcantly\u7b80\u5316\u4e86search space\n2. Multi-resolution optimization: We start with a coarse grid (8 points) and progressively move to finer grids (400 points), projecting and refining solutions at each level\n3. Bayesian optimization with Gaussian Process surrogates: This includes global exploration with Expected Improvement acquisition function and local exploitation\n4. Solution transfer: Solutions from lower resolutions are projected to higher resolutions, combining coarse intuition with fine detail\n\nThis addresses two main limitations of the previous approach:\n1. Local optima trapping: The hierarchical structure helps escape local optima by leveraging lower-resolution information\n2. Slow convergence: The combination of global Bayesian optimization and local refinement accelerates convergence\n\nHowever, implementational challenges include handling different grid sizes, carefully combining coarse-to-fine transfers, and managing computational cost while staying under the time budget.\n\nMathematicalspecification\nWe optimize step functions under the symmetry constraint h(x) + h(2-x) = 1 and integral constraint. The optimization problem is defined as:\n\nmin_{h} C5(h)\nsubject to:\n0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\u2200i\n\u222bh dx = 1\n\nVariables:\n- First half of grid: h[i] for i=0 to m-1, where m = n/2 (n even grid points)\n- The entire grid of n points is constructed by symmetry: h[j] = 1 - h[n-1-j] for j \u2265 m\n\nObjective:\nC5(h) = (2/n) * max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n\u2192 Computed as: max(np.correlate(h_full, 1-h_full, mode='full')) * (2/n)\n\nConstraints handling:\n- Integral: Automatically satisfied by symmetry (for n even)\n- Bounds: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- Symmetry: Enforced by construction, no need for explicit constraint\n\nImplementationprocedure\n1. Initialization:\n   - Set grid levels based on size: [8,16,32,64,128,256,400]\n   - Randomly initialize solution for smallest grid (8 points)\n   - If initial_h_values available, use it for higher-resolutions\n\n2. Core optimization loop:\n   For each grid level (size n_i):\n   a. Define effective variables: m = n_i/2 variables in [0,1]\n   b. Construct full grid h_full using symmetry\n   c. Compute C5 score using full grid correlation\n   d. Use Bayesian optimization (with Sthenno package recommended) with:\n      - Gaussian Process surrogate\n      - Expected Improvement acquisition function\n      - Budget: 30% of remaining time\n   e. Transfer best solution to next finer grid\n   f. Store all-time best solution regardless of grid\n\n3. Refinement techniques:\n   - Bayesian optimization with\u68c4\u75be\u800c\u7528\tregister\n   - Use early termination if score not improving\n   - Apply local refinement with coordinate descent on best solutions\n   - Multi-start optimization at each level:\n\n4. Bayesian optimization details:\n   - Inputs: Gaussian Process surrogate to the function mapping variables to C5\n   - Define acquisition function: Expected Improvement\n   - Number of evaluations: 400 (or more with higher budget)\n   - Bounds: [0,1] for all effective variables\n   - Minimize objective without violate symmetry/feasibility\n\n5. Solution projection:\n   Given solution h_old on grid size old_n, project to new grid size new_n (new_n > old_n):\n   - Resample distribution similarly in terms of mass\n   - Use linear interpolation for function values\n   - Maintain symmetry properties\n\n6. Computationalfallback:\n   - Maintain history of the best solution at each level\n   - In case of exceeding time budget, return:\n     best_h_values, best_score, n_points\n\nSearchchoicesandusefulvariations\n1. Main variations to try:\n   - Projection technique: Linear vs. mass-preserving interpolation\n   - Bayesian optimization settings:\n        * Lengthscale optimization: True\n        * Acquisition function: EI (as default, but also try PI/GPO)\n        * Number of evaluations: [300, 500, 1000]\n        * Max eval per acquisition: 10\n   - Optimization methods:\n        * COBYLA (bounds only)\n        * SLSQP (bounds and equality constraints)\n        * L-BFGS-B (bounds)\n   - Adaptive: Only optimize first half of grid (x=0 to 1) to allow more budget\n\n2. Select these variations sequentially:\n   - Start with linear projection and SLSQP\n   - Only switch to alternative when improvement stalls\n   - Increase number of evaluations after convergence\n   - Change acquisition function after 100 evaluations if plateau\n\n3. Adaptive rule:\n   - Choose projection technique based on variance of scores\n   - Select number of evaluations based on improvement rate\n   - Change optimization method when local convergence detected\n\nComputebudgetandrobustness\n1. Grid size computation:\n   - Levels: [8,16,32,64,128,256,400]\n   - Most expensive level: n=400 (300 function evaluations)\n   - Total potential evaluations: 3500 costly operations\n\n2. Time breakdown:\n   - Resolution increase: 20%\n   - Function evaluations: 80%\n   - Bayesian optimization dominates time\n\n3. Failure recovery:\n   - Preserve history of best solution at each grid\n   - Retain initial solution as fallback if Bayesian optimization fails\n   - Use early stopping for badly performing methods\n\n4. Numerical robustness:\n   - Clip values to [0,1] at each correlation\n   - Handle grid expansions carefully\n   - Validation of integrals at conclusion\n   - Fallback if any grid evaluation exceeds time\n\n5. Recommended parameters:\n   - Max budget s=1300 seconds\n   - Projection factor: 8 (coarsest)\n   - Bayesian acquisitions: 10\n   - Monitoring: stop if scores not improving within 3 acquisitions\n   - Early termination: 80% of budget used but no improvement\n\nValidationandreturncontract\n1. Feasibility checks:\n   - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i (verified by projection)\n   - Symmetry: h[j] + h[n-1-j]\u22481\n   - Integral: \u22481 (should be satisfied by symmetry)\n   - Run time: < budget_s seconds\n\n2. Objective computation:\n   - Compute exactly via: max(np.correlate(h_full, 1-h_full, mode='full')) * (2/n)\n   - Recompute final score from orthogonal projections\n\n3. Output format:\n   - h_values[:-1]: effective variables (first half)\n   - n_points: =400 (always final grid)\n   - C5: score from best solution\n\n4. Consistency checks:\n   - Verify integral: np.sum(h_full)*dx \u22481\n   - Symmetry self-check: np.max(np.abs(h_full[n-1-j]-1+h_full[j])) < 1e-4\n   - Run appropriate integrals at each grid level to validate scores\n\nAny violation of constraints, bounds, or scores is handled by default solution:\n\n   - Random feasible solution\n   - Manual C5 penance for low-quality solutions\n\nImplementationdetails for coder:\n   - Must use specified projection techniques\n   - Must use Gaussian Process Bayesian optimization (scikit-optimize/pkg)\n   - Only use scipy/numpy for numerical arrays\n   - Scale Bayesian optimization appropriately for dimension\n   - Handle grid projections carefully\n   - Preserve parent solution if available\n   - Return formats must exactly match required",
